1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,320
Speaker 1: Hey everybody, Peter Zahn here coming to you from the Chicago Lakeshore.

2
00:00:05,420 --> 00:00:11,500
Speaker 1: Today's topic is China's difficulty in securing energy supplies from really about anywhere.

3
00:00:12,580 --> 00:00:16,660
Speaker 1: The trigger for me for this one is something that happened a couple weeks ago.

4
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:21,820
Speaker 1: We had one of the regional chiefs of a group called TTP, the name is right here,

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,140
Speaker 1: also known as the Pakistani Taliban.

6
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:29,880
Speaker 1: Now, for those of you who have not blocked out the entire 20 years of the war on terror,

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,540
Speaker 1: you'll remember the TTP was one of the groups that really plagued the United States

8
00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:39,600
Speaker 1: in getting supplies from the Guar port in western Pakistan up into Afghanistan.

9
00:00:39,940 --> 00:00:42,160
Speaker 1: A secondary route was going for Karachi North.

10
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:45,500
Speaker 1: TTP controls a lot of the countryside.

11
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:51,580
Speaker 1: A lot of western and especially northern Pakistan is dry to desert and rugged and mountainous.

12
00:00:51,860 --> 00:00:56,840
Speaker 1: And so you can easily have a militant group that just snarls supply lines.

13
00:00:56,920 --> 00:01:02,680
Speaker 1: Anyway, this guy, a couple weeks ago, said that he's perfectly willing to allow the Chinese

14
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:08,360
Speaker 1: to build their massive infrastructure project through Pakistan as long as his tribe gets 5% protection money.

15
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:11,360
Speaker 1: Which, you know, is kind of novel that he's got a business model like that.

16
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:17,360
Speaker 1: Anyway, he means it and he's made it very clear that he and his people are willing to attack

17
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:20,620
Speaker 1: the infrastructure, the construction sites, and the workers.

18
00:01:20,740 --> 00:01:22,260
Speaker 1: He made that very explicit in his statements.

19
00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:24,280
Speaker 1: And it kind of leaves the Chinese reeling.

20
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:29,000
Speaker 1: One of the things that people forget about the Chinese is there's always this weird assumption

21
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,920
Speaker 1: in the West and China is that China is somehow different from all of the powers that have come before.

22
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:38,460
Speaker 1: And if you just introduce a new major power to the area,

23
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:40,980
Speaker 1: everyone's going to just buddy up to them because they're not what came before.

24
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:42,340
Speaker 1: And that's just never true.

25
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:48,380
Speaker 1: The Chinese generate just as much mal-feeling as everybody else.

26
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:51,280
Speaker 1: And they're seen as just another imperial power in most zones.

27
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:55,960
Speaker 1: And for the Chinese, this has always been more of a problem than has been for the Western powers.

28
00:01:55,960 --> 00:02:01,440
Speaker 1: Because if you're Britain or France or Spain or any of the old imperial set,

29
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:06,140
Speaker 1: yes, there's always issues with getting access to energy.

30
00:02:06,780 --> 00:02:09,220
Speaker 1: But you're relatively proximate to a lot of them.

31
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:14,000
Speaker 1: So, for example, if you're, say, just France, you know, Algeria is right across the Mediterranean as is Libya.

32
00:02:14,860 --> 00:02:18,600
Speaker 1: Ah, go around the bulge of Africa and you're in Nigeria.

33
00:02:18,940 --> 00:02:23,380
Speaker 1: Or you can go over to Suez and all of a sudden you're in the Middle East and the Persian Gulf region.

34
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:25,080
Speaker 1: And so there's a crude of plenty.

35
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:27,100
Speaker 1: There's multiple routes to choose from.

36
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:32,720
Speaker 1: And not that any of them are super easy, but it's not like any of them are super complicated.

37
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:37,740
Speaker 1: Even for the United States, back in the days before shale, when we had to import a lot,

38
00:02:38,340 --> 00:02:40,500
Speaker 1: we could go from the West Coast to Southeast Asia.

39
00:02:40,820 --> 00:02:42,900
Speaker 1: We'd go south to Mexico and Latin America.

40
00:02:43,180 --> 00:02:47,880
Speaker 1: Or you'd go east to all those normal places in Africa and the Middle East.

41
00:02:48,300 --> 00:02:49,680
Speaker 1: There were options. There were always options.

42
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:54,980
Speaker 1: And that meant that the United States, even if you ignore the fact that it rules the oceans,

43
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:57,520
Speaker 1: still had options for keeping the lights on.

44
00:02:58,080 --> 00:02:59,260
Speaker 1: Chinese have never had that.

45
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:03,540
Speaker 1: By the time that China expanded to the point that it needed all the energy,

46
00:03:04,040 --> 00:03:05,640
Speaker 1: Southeast Asia was already largely tapped.

47
00:03:05,860 --> 00:03:11,060
Speaker 1: So they need to go around Singapore, by India, and into the Persian Gulf,

48
00:03:11,420 --> 00:03:15,060
Speaker 1: where they have to deal with either the Iranians or the Saudis or the Kuwaitis or whoever else.

49
00:03:15,580 --> 00:03:16,880
Speaker 1: And that's really been the only play.

50
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:19,280
Speaker 1: It has always been the only play.

51
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:22,120
Speaker 1: And that means they've had a hard time with security.

52
00:03:22,760 --> 00:03:30,140
Speaker 1: So we're posting with this video a graphic that was originally in my second book, Absent Superpower,

53
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:32,620
Speaker 1: that we've updated with more current data.

54
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:35,680
Speaker 1: This is the best data we have as of the point that COVID hit.

55
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:37,200
Speaker 1: Because after that, the data is bleh.

56
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:40,180
Speaker 1: It'll be a couple more years before we get post-COVID data that's any good.

57
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:48,300
Speaker 2: And it gives you an idea of just the scale, the volume, and the range that the Chinese have to deal with in order to get fuel.

58
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:56,500
Speaker 2: So starting west to east, you've got the Persian Gulf where they find themselves having to pick sides.

59
00:03:57,040 --> 00:04:01,380
Speaker 1: Everyone thinks that they're going to go with the Iranians because the Saudis have traditionally been more pro-American.

60
00:04:02,080 --> 00:04:08,020
Speaker 1: But Saudis export an order of magnitude almost more crude than the Iranians.

61
00:04:08,220 --> 00:04:13,000
Speaker 1: Not to mention that if you side with the Iranians that you're going to have problems with the Kuwaitis or the Qataris or the Omanis.

62
00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:20,780
Speaker 1: There's no clear answer here, except for to try to replace the United States as the dominant power within the Persian Gulf.

63
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:23,880
Speaker 2: And the Chinese absolutely lack the military reach to do that.

64
00:04:24,740 --> 00:04:29,960
Speaker 2: Then, assuming they can get past all of those local problems, they have to get by India,

65
00:04:30,060 --> 00:04:33,140
Speaker 2: which I don't know if you've been following the news, but for the last 80 years,

66
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:35,660
Speaker 2: the Indians and the Chinese haven't much cared for one another.

67
00:04:36,100 --> 00:04:40,900
Speaker 2: And while the Indian Navy is not great, the Indian Navy operates by India.

68
00:04:41,460 --> 00:04:46,520
Speaker 2: And so there's nothing, nothing that the Chinese could do if it came into a fight to protect their civilian shipping there.

69
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:51,520
Speaker 2: Then they have to get by Singapore, which is a tight American ally,

70
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:54,960
Speaker 2: and each even maintains a berth for American supercarriers.

71
00:04:55,460 --> 00:04:58,680
Speaker 2: They go within range of the missile systems that the Australians have.

72
00:04:59,060 --> 00:05:03,960
Speaker 2: They go by the coast of Vietnam, who hates, hates, hates, hates them so much.

73
00:05:04,660 --> 00:05:06,680
Speaker 2: They go by the Philippines, which is another U.S. ally.

74
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:09,480
Speaker 2: And if you want to get into northern China, you now have to go by Taiwan.

75
00:05:09,740 --> 00:05:13,320
Speaker 2: And again, if you haven't been following the news, Taiwan and China don't get along very well.

76
00:05:13,660 --> 00:05:18,820
Speaker 2: And this whole time, you have to deal with both the American Navy and the Japanese Navy,

77
00:05:18,820 --> 00:05:21,720
Speaker 2: which may have fewer ships, but they have longer range and more lethality.

78
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:25,820
Speaker 2: So there's, there's no solution here on the water.

79
00:05:25,820 --> 00:05:36,880
Speaker 2: The hope with Pakistan is that eventually you can build an infrastructure corridor from Pakistan up over the mountains and into western China,

80
00:05:36,880 --> 00:05:41,400
Speaker 2: where you can at least bypass those latter series of obstacles.

81
00:05:41,880 --> 00:05:47,080
Speaker 2: The problem here is that running a pipe up and over mountain passes is really, really hard.

82
00:05:47,180 --> 00:05:48,620
Speaker 2: That's why no one does it right now.

83
00:05:49,340 --> 00:05:53,500
Speaker 2: And now if you've got local terrorists, they're just deciding to blow everything up.

84
00:05:54,040 --> 00:05:55,440
Speaker 2: It's just one more problem.

85
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:01,860
Speaker 1: So if there was an easy solution here, it would have been found decades ago, but there's not.

86
00:06:01,860 --> 00:06:09,800
Speaker 1: Which means that China remains the most vulnerable power of all of the major powers throughout human history.

87
00:06:10,420 --> 00:06:15,360
Speaker 1: They're completely dependent upon an international security arrangement that they can't protect,

88
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:18,220
Speaker 3: they can't influence, and certainly can't replace.

89
00:06:18,480 --> 00:06:19,440
Speaker 4: Oh, one more thing.

90
00:06:19,780 --> 00:06:22,180
Speaker 4: Well, a lot of this news is bad.

91
00:06:22,260 --> 00:06:23,420
Speaker 4: I mean, very bad for the Chinese.

92
00:06:23,540 --> 00:06:26,420
Speaker 1: In fact, worse than it is for any of the other major powers.

93
00:06:26,560 --> 00:06:27,700
Speaker 1: It's not all negative.

94
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:31,020
Speaker 1: China's problem was with liquid fuels that are derived from oil.

95
00:06:31,020 --> 00:06:35,560
Speaker 1: So jet fuel, gasoline, diesel, things like that.

96
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:38,320
Speaker 1: Those are the things that they're going to lose access to at scale.

97
00:06:39,720 --> 00:06:44,840
Speaker 1: But solid fuels, most notably coal, soft, brown, low-quality coal,

98
00:06:44,940 --> 00:06:47,420
Speaker 1: is something they have in huge volumes at home.

99
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:53,800
Speaker 1: So even in the worst-case scenario for external security situations and energy crises,

100
00:06:54,340 --> 00:06:57,200
Speaker 1: they're still going to be able to produce coal for their own domestic system.

101
00:06:57,340 --> 00:07:00,180
Speaker 1: So if you're willing to put up with just ridiculous carbon emissions

102
00:07:00,180 --> 00:07:01,500
Speaker 1: and atrocious air quality,

103
00:07:01,880 --> 00:07:05,600
Speaker 1: the Chinese do have the ability to keep the lights on.

104
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:08,160
Speaker 1: They can generate electricity for all of their needs.

105
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:11,620
Speaker 1: That's not going to save them from all of the other problems.

106
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:16,360
Speaker 1: But at least they won't be suffering in the cold and in the dark.
